X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2233160-book-talk-reimagining-aid"}
X-SOURCE-ID:iu_hamilton_lugar
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20260401T170000Z
DTEND:20260401T180000Z
LOCATION:Zoom
SUMMARY:Book Talk: Reimagining Aid
DESCRIPTION:Reimagining Aid uses ethnographic research in Cambodia to exa
 mine how aid from Japan and the United States shapes women's health initi
 atives. The book highlights how Cambodian development actors are not just
  aid recipients but active participants who reshape development by blendi
 ng regional Asian perspectives with Western influences. It ultimately cal
 ls for a rethinking of development and gender policies in an increasingly
  multipolar world.  Sponsored by the O'Neill School of Public and Environ
 mental Affairs and the Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International 
 Studies Tobias Center for Innovation in International Development.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><em>Reimagining Aid</em> uses ethnographi
 c research in Cambodia to examine how aid from Japan and the United State
 s shapes women's health initiatives. The book highlights how Cambodian de
 velopment actors are not just aid recipients but active participants who 
 reshape development by blending regional Asian perspectives with Western 
 influences. It ultimately calls for a rethinking of development and gende
 r policies in an increasingly multipolar world.</p><p>  Sponsored by the 
 O'Neill School of Public and Environmental Affairs and the Hamilton Lugar
  School of Global and International Studies Tobias Center for Innovation 
 in International Development.</p>
UID:20260401T170000Z-2234259@events.iu.edu
DTSTAMP:20260310T111807Z
