X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2012256-21jpsi-speaker-series-the-role-of-dialect-in-contemp"}
X-SOURCE-ID:iu_hamilton_lugar
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20260403T160000Z
DTEND:20260403T170000Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING - Room Number: 1112
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
SUMMARY:21JPSI/EASC: "The Role of Dialect in Contemporary Japanese Societ
 y"(Prof. Jae Takeuchi\; IU)
DESCRIPTION:Talk abstract: Japanese dialects continue to be used in regio
 ns across Japan\, despite the promotion of Standard Japanese through educ
 ation and media. Drawing on examples from Ehime Prefecture\, this present
 ation will discuss how Japanese dialects are evolving and consider what t
 hey contribute to the linguistic landscape of contemporary Japan.Jae DiBe
 llo Takeuchi is Associate Professor of Japanese in the Department of East
  Asian Languages and Cultures at Indiana University. Dr. Takeuchi conduct
 s research in Japanese sociolinguistics and applied linguistics\, second 
 language acquisition\, and foreign language pedagogy. Her research focuse
 s on the experiences of Japanese second language speakers who are long-te
 rm residents of Japan. She is particularly interested in how they navigat
 e Japanese speech styles\, such as dialects and keigo\, which they encoun
 ter in local communities\, workplaces\, and leisure activities. At Indian
 a University\, Dr. Takeuchi teaches courses in Japanese language and soci
 olinguistics. Before coming to Indiana\, she taught Japanese at Clemson U
 niversity and served as the director of the Languages and International B
 usiness program.  Dr. Takeuchi's 2023 book Language Ideologies and L2 Spe
 aker Legitimacy: Native Speaker Bias in Japan examines native speaker bia
 s and L2 speaker legitimacy. Her recent projects focus on linguistic micr
 oaggression and consider how speaker identity and markers such as accent 
 impact L2 speakers' acceptance in Japanese communities.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><em><span style="text-decoration: underli
 ne\;"><strong>Talk abstract:</strong></span> Japanese dialects continue t
 o be used in regions across Japan\, despite the promotion of Standard Jap
 anese through education and media. Drawing on examples from Ehime Prefect
 ure\, this presentation will discuss how Japanese dialects are evolving a
 nd consider what they contribute to the linguistic landscape of contempor
 ary Japan.</em></p><p><strong>Jae DiBello Takeuchi</strong> is Associate 
 Professor of Japanese in the Department of East Asian Languages and Cultu
 res at Indiana University. Dr. Takeuchi conducts research in Japanese soc
 iolinguistics and applied linguistics\, second language acquisition\, and
  foreign language pedagogy. Her research focuses on the experiences of Ja
 panese second language speakers who are long-term residents of Japan. She
  is particularly interested in how they navigate Japanese speech styles\,
  such as dialects and <em>keigo</em>\, which they encounter in local comm
 unities\, workplaces\, and leisure activities. At Indiana University\, Dr
 . Takeuchi teaches courses in Japanese language and sociolinguistics. Bef
 ore coming to Indiana\, she taught Japanese at Clemson University and ser
 ved as the director of the Languages and International Business program.<
 /p><p>  Dr. Takeuchi's 2023 book <em>Language Ideologies and L2 Speaker L
 egitimacy: Native Speaker Bias in Japan</em> examines native speaker bias
  and L2 speaker legitimacy. Her recent projects focus on linguistic micro
 aggression and consider how speaker identity and markers such as accent i
 mpact L2 speakers' acceptance in Japanese communities.</p>
