BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2253411-housing-policy-and-prospects-of-upward-mobility"}
X-SOURCE-ID:iu_hamilton_lugar
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20260410T160000Z
DTEND:20260410T173000Z
LOCATION:WOODBURN HALL - Room Number: 005
GEO:39.168107;-86.5201
SUMMARY:Housing Policy and Prospects of Upward Mobility: Evidence from So
 uth Korea
DESCRIPTION:Rising wealth inequality and worsening housing crises have in
 tensified economic grievances\, raising urgent questions about how access
  to housing carries political implications.  Soonhwan Kwon examines this 
 question by focusing on its role in shaping both economic security and we
 alth accumulation.   The study argues that policies expanding access to h
 omeownership—such as easing credit\, transaction\, and tax constraints 
 in the housing market—reshape individuals' prospects of upward mobility
 . Using a 2023 housing market deregulation in Seoul\, South Korea\, the p
 aper shows that these policies increase upward mobility prospects among h
 omeowners and higher-income renters\, while reducing them among lower-inc
 ome renters. These findings suggest that housing policy shapes not only e
 conomic inequality but also how individuals interpret their economic posi
 tion and the role of housing in wealth accumulation\, with unequal politi
 cal implications across socioeconomic groups.Soonhwan Kwon is a Ph.D. stu
 dent in Political Science at Indiana University Bloomington\, specializin
 g in comparative politics and political methodology. His research examine
 s how housing policies reshape prospects of upward mobility and beliefs a
 bout housing as a source of wealth\, and how these processes relate to un
 equal political outcomes across socioeconomic groups.   A light lunch wil
 l be provided!&#160\;
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Rising wealth inequality and worsening 
 housing crises have intensified economic grievances\, raising urgent ques
 tions about how access to housing carries political implications.</p><p> 
  Soonhwan Kwon examines this question by focusing on its role in shaping 
 both economic security and wealth accumulation. <strong></strong></p><p> 
  The study argues that policies expanding access to homeownership—such 
 as easing credit\, transaction\, and tax constraints in the housing marke
 t—reshape individuals' prospects of upward mobility. Using a 2023 housi
 ng market deregulation in Seoul\, South Korea\, the paper shows that thes
 e policies increase upward mobility prospects among homeowners and higher
 -income renters\, while reducing them among lower-income renters. These f
 indings suggest that housing policy shapes not only economic inequality b
 ut also how individuals interpret their economic position and the role of
  housing in wealth accumulation\, with unequal political implications acr
 oss socioeconomic groups.</p><p><i></i></p><p><i>Soonhwan Kwon is a Ph.D.
  student in Political Science at Indiana University Bloomington\, special
 izing in comparative politics and political methodology. His research exa
 mines how housing policies reshape prospects of upward mobility and belie
 fs about housing as a source of wealth\, and how these processes relate t
 o unequal political outcomes across socioeconomic groups. </i></p><p>  A 
 light lunch will be provided!&#160\;</p>
UID:20260410T160000Z-2254482@events.iu.edu
DTSTAMP:20260331T091356Z
