BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2247252--straight-talk-russian-language-colloquium-belarus"}
X-SOURCE-ID:iu_hamilton_lugar
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20260416T160000Z
DTEND:20260416T170000Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING - Room Number: 4067
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
SUMMARY:Прямая речь!/Straight Talk! Russian Language Colloquium
 : "Belarus 101: Crossroad of Europe" with Ilya Silich
DESCRIPTION:This lecture introduces Belarus as a historical crossroads of
 ten overlooked in Western narratives. Through a series of vivid snapshots
 \, it explores unexpected connections from Gothic migrations and Viking t
 rade routes to religious diversity in the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth.
  It highlights Jewish intellectual life\, Protestant movements\, and mult
 icultural communities\, as well as links to American history\, including 
 migration and industrial exchange. It traces how these legacies shape a m
 odern tension between preserved Soviet legacy like collective farms and K
 GB and a highly developed digital market forced out of the country. The l
 ecture concludes by explaining contemporary authoritarianism\, exiled gov
 ernment in the 21st century\, protests\, political prisoners\, and why U.
 S. engagement has played a tangible role in recent developments.Ilya Sili
 ch is a Belarusian activist-in-exile\, IT specialist\, and former elected
  representative in the digital grassroots democracy initiative "Сход"
  (Assembly) in Brest. He supervised volunteers and monitored elections in
  2020 as a member of civic engagement initiative "Честные Люди
 " (Honest People). Ilya hold degrees in art studies from the Belarusian S
 tate Academy of Arts. He is currently an independent researcher with a fo
 cus on civil society\, political transformation\, and national identity.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><span style="margin-top: 1em\; margin-bot
 tom: 1em\;" data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">This lecture introduces Be
 larus as a historical crossroads often overlooked in Western narratives. 
 Through a series of vivid snapshots\, it explores unexpected connections 
 from Gothic migrations and Viking trade routes to religious diversity in 
 the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. It highlights Jewish intellectual lif
 e\, Protestant movements\, and multicultural communities\, as well as lin
 ks to American history\, including migration and industrial exchange. It 
 traces how these legacies shape a modern tension between preserved Soviet
  legacy like collective farms and KGB and a highly developed digital mark
 et forced out of the country. The lecture concludes by explaining contemp
 orary authoritarianism\, exiled government in the 21st century\, protests
 \, political prisoners\, and why U.S. engagement has played a tangible ro
 le in recent developments.</span></p><p><span style="margin-top: 1em\; ma
 rgin-bottom: 1em\;" data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">Ilya Silich is a B
 elarusian activist-in-exile\, IT specialist\, and former elected represen
 tative in the digital grassroots democracy initiative "Сход" (Assembl
 y) in Brest. He supervised volunteers and monitored elections in 2020 as 
 a member of civic engagement initiative "Честные Люди" (Honest
  People). Ilya hold degrees in art studies from the Belarusian State Acad
 emy of Arts. He is currently an independent researcher with a focus on ci
 vil society\, political transformation\, and national identity.</span></p
 >
